<p>U.S. cybersecurity, law enforcement, and intelligence agencies warned on April 7 that Iran-linked hacking campaigns are escalating against equipment used in U.S. critical infrastructure. They urged municipalities and operators to watch for unusual activity that could affect water and energy services. The alert underscores a growing risk that hostilities in the Middle East could spill into critical systems far from the battlefield. Officials said the advisory is meant to help utilities and local governments strengthen defenses before disruptions occur.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/TaGpS5tL1maLzmCroA_QYeyqM9OlEeSFbwnymbBMIj4mgXfiBd2hV_gx8xKLGawxnYZt5DXOQXypnGSAkLimS-2orIHE11UqK2_pfEocU4JhQPWAKWx8aUfVEBI4QhqJfk2SdxYlTu6s3032WjQsJFy7_m3DEP3udDUYhsayk26a4VBXpPpQR7KccM7HmD1eGoN3Uzb1fqG8FN5SHVhq0l6jbgwv1p3l' alt='Energy systems across the United States may be the target of cyberattacks, according to the Trump administration.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Conflict-linked spike: Agencies said the hacking campaigns have intensified since the war began, directly linking the activity to current hostilities.</li><li>Cross-sector exposure: The advisory covered equipment used across multiple U.S. critical infrastructure sectors, suggesting the risk reaches beyond the water and energy systems highlighted in headlines.</li><li>Disclosure limits: The public warning did not name specific facilities that were hit or say whether any damage occurred.</li></ul><blockquote>Cyberattacks on drinking water and wastewater systems directly threaten public health and community resilience. A single breach can disrupt treatment or introduce contaminants, damage equipment, and erode public trust. - Jeffrey Hall</blockquote><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iranian-hackers-targeting-of-us-critical-infrastructure-has-escalated-since-start-of-war-us-says'>Iranian hackers&#x27; targeting of US critical infrastructure has escalated since